  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nHigh pressure crossing into Saturday settles toward central Plains\nand Ohio Valley by Sunday. Patchy ground fog possible beneath the\nhigh tonight into early Saturday. Airmass very dry so not expecting\nmuch coverage to fog, but with temps below freezing and pavement \ntemps dropping below freezing up north, a few slippery spots could\noccur. \n\nCold front (more of a wind shift) sweeping through late Saturday \nnight into Sunday along with pressure rises and winds up to 35 kts\nin the mixed layer may push wind gusts 30-35 mph over the northwoods,\nwith 20-30 mph elsewhere. \n\nHighs on Sunday reach the lower to middle 50s, which is back \nabove average. Warmer temperatures continue on Monday and Tuesday,\nwith highs maybe reaching 60. However, probabilities for highs \nreaching above 60F have diminished compared to what was shown last\nnight. Stronger cold front tied to broad upper troughing drops \nacross the region on Wednesday, Veterans Day. Low chances for rain\nnow showing up in the forecast on into Thursday, matching what \nwas shown by LREF data yesterday. Not a widespread signal for \nprecip, mainly just impacting lake effect areas of north-central \nand along Door County/Lake Michigan. Even on the LREF probabilities\nfor more than 0.25 inch of precip are less than 5%, so if this \noccurs it looks pretty light. Hint of colder air is showing up \nmore now into late next week, so seems could see some rain/snow or\nsnow at times for the lake effect areas of the north as 850mb \ntemps drop to around -8c. During this most recent cool down, \n850mb temps were -4c to -6c, so could end up even cooler later \nnext week. Extended MOS guidance from GFS and ECMWF points to this\nas well.",
